(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-ent-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"ENT Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which vascular source accounts for anterior epistaxis, and what proportion of cases is that?","choices":["Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall, about 90 per cent","Kiesselbach's plexus on the anterior septum, about 90 per cent","The sphenopalatine artery, about 50 per cent","The anterior ethmoidal artery, about 10 per cent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nine in ten nosebleeds are anterior and come from this plexus, which is why direct compression works so often. The posterior 10 per cent come from Woodruff's plexus on the posterolateral wall. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does cerumen impaction cause conductive rather than sensorineural loss?","choices":["It blocks the canal, so sound never reaches the conducting chain","It damages the outer hair cells","It stiffens the stapes footplate","It loads the tympanic membrane with fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cochlea is untouched, and the defect is purely one of delivery. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In conductive hearing loss, which way does Weber lateralise and what does Rinne show?","choices":["Weber to the AFFECTED ear; Rinne shows bone conduction greater than air","Weber to the UNAFFECTED ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber to the affected ear; Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone","Weber is equal; Rinne shows bone greater than air bilaterally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The blocked ear is not receiving competing air-borne sound, so bone-conducted sound is heard more loudly in it — and with the conducting chain impaired, bone beats air on Rinne. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which history most separates labyrinthitis from Meniere disease?","choices":["Unilateral rather than bilateral symptoms","The presence of tinnitus","A recent upper respiratory infection","The presence of sensorineural hearing loss"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Labyrinthitis usually follows a viral infection, so a cold last week points hard at it; someone who has not had a cold in years is likelier to have Meniere disease. Both can be unilateral, so laterality does not separate them. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where do the pressure waves that begin hearing actually travel first?","choices":["The scala tympani","The cochlear duct itself","The scala vestibuli","The semicircular canals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stapes movement at the oval window starts them there, and they displace endolymph in the cochlear duct. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What protects the outer third of the ear canal?","choices":["Mucus from goblet cells","Ciliated epithelium sweeping debris outward","A keratin plug at the isthmus","Cerumen from ceruminous glands, and hair follicles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both sit in the outer third, which is why impaction and otitis externa are canal diseases. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How do vocal cord polyps differ from nodules in site and pathogenesis?","choices":["Bilateral fibrous calluses from chronic phonotrauma","Bilateral soft lesions at the anterior commissure from reflux","Unilateral, soft and pedunculated on the middle third, after one severe strain","Unilateral fibrous calluses from chronic yelling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One event rather than chronic repetition, and the healing response is inflammatory rather than a callus. Site and laterality both differ from nodules. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What kind of immune reaction is allergic rhinitis, and what does it look like?","choices":["IgG-mediated type 2 hypersensitivity with purulent rhinorrhoea","Immune complex deposition, type 3 hypersensitivity","T cell mediated, type 4 delayed hypersensitivity","IgE-mediated type 1 hypersensitivity, with CLEAR rhinorrhoea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type 1, immediate, IgE-driven. The CLEAR discharge is what separates it at the bedside from bacterial sinusitis, which is purulent. Allergic shiners complete the picture. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which physical law explains why a small septal deviation causes disproportionate obstruction?","choices":["Fick's law of diffusion","Laplace's law","Boyle's law","Poiseuille's law"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Resistance rises with the fourth power of the radius, so a modest narrowing has an outsized effect on airflow. That is why a deviation that looks minor can produce persistent obstruction. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A temporal bone fracture causes conductive loss by which mechanism?","choices":["Discontinuity of the ossicular chain","Stiffness of the ossicular chain","Mass loading of the tympanic membrane","Obstruction of the external canal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The chain is physically disrupted so the wave cannot be conducted across it. Ossicular necrosis produces the same end result by erosion rather than trauma. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which causes are listed as CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo, Meniere disease and labyrinthitis","Brainstem stroke, multiple sclerosis and cerebellar tumour","Otosclerosis and cerumen impaction","Presbycusis and noise trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All involve the brainstem or cerebellum, which is why the nystagmus can be vertical and is not suppressed by fixation, and why other neurological signs appear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of labyrinthitis, and why does it impair hearing AND balance together?","choices":["Defective endolymph resorption ballooning the scala media","Dislodged otoconia striking structures in the endolymph","Fixation of the stapes footplate","Inflammation across both the semicircular canals and the cochlea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The inflammation does not respect the boundary between the vestibular apparatus and the organ of Corti, so both fail at once. That simultaneous involvement is what names it otitis interna. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why are there three semicircular canals rather than one?","choices":["Each detects a different speed of rotation","Two detect rotation and one detects gravity","Redundancy in case one is damaged","They sit in three planes, so rotation in any direction is detected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sagittal, coronal and transverse between them cover all rotational movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does it mean, structurally, for lymph nodes to be MATTED?","choices":["They have lost their individual capsules and move as one mass","They are tender and mobile","They are smaller than normal","They fluctuate in size with infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tissue no longer respecting its own boundaries is why matting raises concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which organisms most commonly cause bacterial otitis media?","choices":["Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus niger and Candida albicans","Group A Streptococcus alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic middle ear trio. Viral causes are respiratory syncytial virus, rhinovirus, influenza and adenovirus. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What enlarges the inferior turbinates, and what does rhinitis medicamentosa refer to?","choices":["Cartilage displacement off the midline; a congenital deformity","Venous engorgement, oedema or bony hypertrophy; decongestant rebound","Eosinophilic polyp formation; an allergic reaction","Bacterial infection of the turbinate bone; osteomyelitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three possible substrates, and the drug-induced one has its own name. It is the same rebound phenomenon that appears in ocular pharmacology &mdash; overuse of a vasoconstrictor leaving the tissue more congested than before. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the bone remodelling sequence in otosclerosis?","choices":["Osteoblastic overgrowth of dense cortical bone without resorption","Osteoclastic resorption, then spongy osteoid replacement","Demineralisation of the ossicles from chronic inflammation","Fibrous scarring of the round window membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bone is removed and replaced by soft vascular osteoid around the otic capsule and stapes footplate. The remodelled bone then fixes the footplate. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which Eustachian tube dysfunction creates negative middle ear pressure?","choices":["The tube fails to open, so the mucosa absorbs the gases","Mucus production increases faster than the tube can drain it","Bacterial infection erodes the tube lining and opens it permanently","The tympanic membrane becomes stiff and pulls the ossicles inward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The middle ear is an air-filled space; if it is not re-ventilated, the mucosa absorbs the gases and the pressure falls. That negative pressure is the start of the otitis media sequence. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is canalithiasis?","choices":["Calcification of the stapes footplate","Dislodged otoconia floating free in the semicircular canals","Accumulation of endolymph in the membranous labyrinth","Inflammatory debris within the cochlea after infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The crystals normally sit in the utricle. Once loose in the endolymph they strike structures and generate a false sensation of motion. Why they dislodge is not the examinable part &mdash; that they can is. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which structures detect linear acceleration and gravitational position?","choices":["The three semicircular canals","The otolith organs","The cupula and ampulla","The organ of Corti"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They handle static and linear balance, while the semicircular canals handle rotation. The two systems together give the brain a complete picture of head position and movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the classic symptom tetrad of Meniere disease?","choices":["Continuous vertigo lasting days, unilateral tinnitus, unilateral loss, and nystagmus","Vertigo lasting under a minute on head movement, with no hearing loss","High-frequency hearing loss with tinnitus and no vertigo","Episodic vertigo for hours, low-frequency tinnitus, low-tone loss, aural fullness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All four, and the LOW-frequency emphasis is what distinguishes the hearing loss from presbycusis or noise damage, which take the high frequencies first. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which feature of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo separates it most cleanly from the other peripheral causes?","choices":["The vertigo lasts several hours","It follows a viral upper respiratory infection","There is NO hearing loss","It produces aural fullness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Meniere disease and labyrinthitis both take hearing with them; this does not, because the otoconia are a vestibular problem that never touches the cochlea. Attacks also last under a minute and follow head position. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do gentamicin and tobramycin damage hearing, and which frequencies go first?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in the stria vascularis, disturbing endolymph ion homeostasis","Reactive oxygen species destroy outer hair cells from the base, so high pitches go first","They alter the stria vascularis potential reversibly","They inhibit the prestin motor protein in outer hair cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The base of the cochlea encodes high frequencies, so basal damage shows as high-frequency loss. The mechanism is oxidative injury, not a direct toxic binding. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are vocal cord nodules, and what causes them?","choices":["Unilateral pedunculated fluid-filled growths on the middle third, from a single severe strain","Bilateral wart-like exophytic lesions from viral infection","Unilateral firm masses with cord fixation, from malignancy","Bilateral symmetrical calluses at the anterior third junction, from chronic phonotrauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Repeated slamming of the cords causes basement membrane hyalinisation &mdash; literally a callus. Bilateral and symmetrical, because both cords take the same repeated impact at the same point. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Cerumen impaction, foreign bodies and canal exostoses all cause conductive loss by which of the four core mechanisms?","choices":["Obstruction","Mass loading","Stiffness effect","Discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A physical blockage stops sound penetrating down the canal. It is the first of four mechanisms, and the only one that acts before the sound reaches the drum. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Is the vertigo of labyrinthitis episodic or continuous, and how long does it run?","choices":["Continuous, over days, improving across weeks","Episodic, with attacks lasting several hours","Episodic, with attacks lasting under a minute","Continuous, permanent and non-improving"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Someone comes in saying the room has been spinning for three days, not that it comes and goes. Continuous vertigo that slowly settles over weeks is the labyrinthitis pattern, because the body is resolving an inflammatory process. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 17 (slide says episodic; corrected in lecture)"},{"q":"In the organ of Corti, what physically opens the tip-link channels on the hair cells?","choices":["Direct pressure of endolymph on the hair cell body","Glutamate binding to the hair cell","Shearing of the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane","Stapes vibration transmitted directly to the hair cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Basilar membrane displacement bends the stereocilia against the rigid tectorial membrane, and that mechanical deflection is what opens the channels. Everything upstream exists to produce that shear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which features mark vertigo as PERIPHERAL rather than central?","choices":["Gradual onset, vertical nystagmus, non-suppressible, with neurological deficits","Gradual onset with hearing loss and tinnitus","Sudden onset, horizontal or rotational nystagmus, fatigable, suppressed by visual fixation","Absence of nystagmus entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All four point at the inner ear or cranial nerve VIII. Suppression by visual fixation is the one most easily tested at the bedside. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why does a deviated septum cause epistaxis and loss of smell?","choices":["Compression of the ethmoid arteries and the olfactory nerve","Turbulent airflow dries the mucosa; air misses the cribriform plate","Chronic infection eroding the septal vessels and olfactory epithelium","Autoimmune destruction of the septal cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Two separate consequences of the same disturbed airflow: local drying with fragile vessel breakdown on one hand, and failure to deliver odorant to the superior nasal vault on the other. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why does a lesion of cranial nerve VIII produce the same audiometric pattern as cochlear damage?","choices":["Both stiffen the ossicles","Both obstruct the canal","Both sit beyond the conducting chain, so both give sensorineural loss","Both add mass to the tympanic membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The category is defined by where the defect sits, not by which of the two structures failed. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
